WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.835 --> 00:00:02.426
This is a mould, cut open.

2
00:00:03.177 --> 00:00:07.682
On the left the fixed half, against the nozzle; on the right the moving half,

3
00:00:07.775 --> 00:00:08.634
which opens.

4
00:00:09.361 --> 00:00:11.102
They meet at the parting line.

5
00:00:12.106 --> 00:00:16.239
The nozzle pushes the melt into the sprue: a channel that widens inwards,

6
00:00:16.402 --> 00:00:18.143
so the set piece can come out.

7
00:00:19.215 --> 00:00:22.884
Runners along the parting line share it between the two cavities.

8
00:00:23.604 --> 00:00:28.770
The gate is narrow on purpose: it freezes first and leaves only a small mark.

9
00:00:29.814 --> 00:00:31.625
The air escapes through the vents:

10
00:00:31.811 --> 00:00:34.667
slots one to five hundredths of a millimetre deep,

11
00:00:34.876 --> 00:00:37.581
depending on the plastic, too shallow for the melt.

12
00:00:38.332 --> 00:00:40.921
Cooling water draws the heat out of the steel.

13
00:00:41.938 --> 00:00:46.153
Then the mould opens, and an ejector pin pushes the part off the core.

14
00:00:46.898 --> 00:00:50.427
A simple mould costs from about twenty thousand euros.

15
00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:55.409
What sits in the sprue and the runners sets too: a cold runner.

16
00:00:56.147 --> 00:00:59.386
It comes out with every shot, as waste or regrind.

17
00:01:00.445 --> 00:01:03.916
With a hot runner, the fixed half holds a heated manifold.

18
00:01:04.708 --> 00:01:07.471
In it the melt stays molten up to the cavity.

19
00:01:08.437 --> 00:01:11.363
So no runner waste, and often a shorter cycle.

20
00:01:12.066 --> 00:01:16.443
But the mould costs more, and a heater zone can burn out or the manifold leak.

21
00:01:17.725 --> 00:01:21.231
Building a new mould typically takes 10 to 16 weeks.

22
00:01:22.004 --> 00:01:26.486
Then comes T1, the first trial, and after corrections T2,

23
00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:29.145
T3, until the customer approves.

24
00:01:30.176 --> 00:01:34.065
On the mould sits a shot counter, which adds one for every cycle.

25
00:01:35.098 --> 00:01:37.629
You plan preventive maintenance on that count,

26
00:01:37.826 --> 00:01:42.853
typically every fifty to a hundred thousand shots: clean the vents, check the pins,

27
00:01:43.108 --> 00:01:44.292
descale the channels.

28
00:01:45.013 --> 00:01:46.836
Skip it, and you get flash.

29
00:01:47.847 --> 00:01:50.784
A mould change typically takes 1 to 2 hours.

30
00:01:51.506 --> 00:01:56.348
With SMED, often 15 to 30 minutes: pre-heating, standard clamp plates,

31
00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:57.520
quick couplings.

32
00:01:58.543 --> 00:02:03.233
Say 40 changes a week, from 90 to 30 minutes: 40 hours freed,

33
00:02:03.419 --> 00:02:07.413
at 60 euros an hour two thousand four hundred euros a week.

34
00:02:08.735 --> 00:02:12.462
An edge gate sits on the parting line; it has to be cut off.

35
00:02:13.244 --> 00:02:17.471
This mould has a tunnel gate, drilled at an angle below the parting line.

36
00:02:18.488 --> 00:02:23.027
As the mould opens and ejects, the steel shears the gate off by itself.

37
00:02:24.022 --> 00:02:29.072
Two parts of 18 grams and a runner of 6: a shot is 42 grams,

38
00:02:29.235 --> 00:02:31.000
and 14 percent is runner.

39
00:02:31.724 --> 00:02:35.416
If 15 percent regrind may go back, it just fits.

40
00:02:36.514 --> 00:02:39.858
Cooling channels are drilled through the plates; in a tall core,

41
00:02:40.008 --> 00:02:42.957
a baffle in the bore leads the water up and down.

42
00:02:44.277 --> 00:02:47.192
When the mould opens, the part stays on the core.

43
00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:51.710
The pins push on the inside, where their round marks don’t show.

44
00:02:52.742 --> 00:02:56.225
Every wall in the opening direction gets a draft angle,

45
00:02:56.353 --> 00:03:00.788
often about 1 degree per side, so the part releases without scraping.

46
00:03:01.512 --> 00:03:03.381
A textured surface needs more.

47
00:03:04.378 --> 00:03:08.442
But this side hole for a clip is an undercut: it blocks straight release,

48
00:03:08.592 --> 00:03:09.591
like a snap hook.

49
00:03:10.601 --> 00:03:12.145
So a slide forms the hole:

50
00:03:12.308 --> 00:03:16.395
a steel block that an angled pin pulls aside as the mould opens.

51
00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:18.865
Only then do the pins push.

52
00:03:19.598 --> 00:03:21.955
Every slide costs money and wears.

53
00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.486
Turn the shot counter into a calendar.

54
00:03:26.206 --> 00:03:29.421
A cap mould: 8 cavities, 12 seconds a shot,

55
00:03:29.596 --> 00:03:33.543
120 hours a week: thirty-six thousand shots.

56
00:03:34.218 --> 00:03:39.931
Service every hundred thousand is every 2.8 weeks, ideally at an order change.

57
00:03:40.943 --> 00:03:45.517
Wear parts wear out predictably: ejector pins, wear plates, seals,

58
00:03:45.668 --> 00:03:46.701
hot-runner heaters.

59
00:03:47.401 --> 00:03:49.352
Keep the critical ones in stock.

60
00:03:50.422 --> 00:03:53.742
The mould logbook records each repair with the shot count.

61
00:03:54.498 --> 00:03:57.157
The same slide every thirty thousand shots?

62
00:03:57.924 --> 00:03:59.468
Then the problem is the design.

63
00:04:00.513 --> 00:04:04.994
When the mould comes off, the last parts go with it: the last-shot sample.

64
00:04:05.743 --> 00:04:09.109
So flash is repaired on the bench, not during start-up.

65
00:04:10.467 --> 00:04:14.345
Internal setup can only happen with the press stopped: water off,

66
00:04:14.531 --> 00:04:16.342
unclamp, crane the mould out.

67
00:04:17.456 --> 00:04:21.763
External setup can happen while the previous order runs: pre-heating,

68
00:04:22.007 --> 00:04:24.549
laying out hoses, drying the material.

69
00:04:25.546 --> 00:04:29.854
Film a change, time each step and move internal work to external.

70
00:04:30.546 --> 00:04:33.367
The measure: last good part to first good part.

71
00:04:34.188 --> 00:04:36.882
More on this in the Capacity and flow module.

72
00:04:38.176 --> 00:04:42.263
In DFM, toolmaker and moulder review the part: even walls,

73
00:04:42.402 --> 00:04:45.920
ribs of 40 to 60 percent of the wall, enough draft.

74
00:04:46.943 --> 00:04:50.914
A fill simulation computes the filling before the steel exists.

75
00:04:51.909 --> 00:04:57.911
The SPI class sets the mould’s life: class 101 lasts a million cycles or more,

76
00:04:58.016 --> 00:05:01.255
class 103 under five hundred thousand.

77
00:05:01.989 --> 00:05:08.572
For 1.5 million parts from 2 cavities, you build 103 twice: six cents a part,

78
00:05:08.735 --> 00:05:10.918
against five for 101.

79
00:05:11.945 --> 00:05:15.115
T0 is the first shot at the toolmaker: does it work?

80
00:05:15.858 --> 00:05:18.679
T1 is the first trial with measured parts.

81
00:05:19.664 --> 00:05:25.724
In automotive, PPAP follows: at least 300 consecutive parts at production rate,

82
00:05:25.933 --> 00:05:29.439
unless the customer agrees otherwise, with the documents.
